- Review title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
- Objective: Assess effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children with HIV 
infection, and in infants of HIV-infected mothers.
- Background:
  - Most HIV-infected children are in low-income countries without ART access.
  - Prevention/early treatment of opportunistic infections is key.
  - Cotrimoxazole is inexpensive, broadly effective (including against Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia), and relatively 
safe.
  - HIV diagnosis in infants is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is one 
approach.
  - Routine prophylaxis is challenging in low-resource settings and may increase resistance.
